  "body": "Original report: crbug.com/1429585\r\n\r\nAt [one place](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#form-associated-custom-elements), the spec text says the callback should be called with state:\r\n\r\n> Each [form-associated custom element](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#form-associated-custom-element) has **state**. It is information with which the user agent can restore a user's input for the element.\r\n\r\n> When the user agent believes it is a good idea to restore a [form-associated custom element](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#form-associated-custom-element)'s [state](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#face-state), for example [after navigation](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/browsing-the-web.html#restore-persisted-state) or restarting the user agent, they may [enqueue a custom element callback reaction](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#enqueue-a-custom-element-callback-reaction) with that element, callback name \"formStateRestoreCallback\", an argument list containing the state to be restored, and \"restore\".\r\n\r\nAt [another place](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#custom-element-reactions), it says the callback should be called with value:\r\n\r\n> When user agent updates a form-associated custom element's value on behalf of a user or as part of navigation, its formStateRestoreCallback is called, given the new value and a string indicating a reason, \"autocomplete\" or \"restore\", as arguments.\r\n\r\nRight now Chromium is calling the callback with value, while WebKit is calling with state.\r\n\r\nFrom my understanding, I think the second place is a typo, and we should call it with state. Is that correct?",
